WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.560
Your car began its life billions of years
ago.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:06.650
Billions of miles away in deep space.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:10.220
The things that make up cars.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:14.450
Those atoms, most of them were forged well
before our Earth was born.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.410
You think your car is a clunker?

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:23.630
It's actually 13.8 billion years old.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.100
All right, Let's do it, sir.

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.750
So what's the stuff that cars are made of?

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.820
Best way to find out.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.530
Tear one apart.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:45.340
Iron, plastics, oils and rubber are the
first to go.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.470
Another half hour or so, this baby is going
to be completely stripped.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:53.460
I can't wait to see it.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:01:02.500
Then aluminum, silicon, copper and finally
precious metals like platinum and gold.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:10.540
Each of these materials is crucial for
building a car.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.660
But in the earliest days of the universe,
none of them existed.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.580
13.8 billion years ago.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.820
Time and space are created in the Big Bang.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:39.310
The early universe is filled with nothing
but energy.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.590
After the Big Bang, it was just a chaotic
glob of stuff.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.270
Nothing like what you see today.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:53.380
As the early universe cools, the energy gives
way to unstable matter and antimatter.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.840
Then protons and neutrons.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.120
And finally atoms.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.270
But none of them are iron, silicon or
carbon.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:05.650
These vast, gassy clouds are mostly
hydrogen.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:14.840
Something had to happen to give us everything
else and everything was actually made from

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:16.340
hydrogen building blocks.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:24.560
An atom of hydrogen is the simplest and
lightest atom in the universe, just a single,

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.860
positively charged proton bound to a single
electron.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:36.810
The universe then builds up bigger atoms
like carbon and iron by fusing hydrogen atoms

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:37.650
together.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.530
Everything starts from simpler origins.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:45.780
An iron atom is actually lots and lots of
simple hydrogen atoms that were stuck

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:46.440
together.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:52.540
At first, this versatile proton building
block doesn't want to stick.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:58.360
Protons are positively charged, so as you
push them closer together, they're going to

00:02:58.360 --> 00:02:59.650
resist coming closer together.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.210
They really don't want to hang out.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:08.590
This repulsion makes the early universe a
maelstrom of hydrogen atoms swerving to avoid

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:09.250
each other.

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:14.680
But if you can get them to a point where you
can shove them together enough, at some point

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.600
you're going to lock together.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:30.410
Pushing atoms together so strongly they stick
is called nuclear fusion.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:36.230
It's the first key step for turning a
universe full of gas into one filled with the

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:40.160
ingredients for people, cars and planets.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:44.600
So how do you get two atoms to fuse?

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.680
This guy's been doing it in his garage since
he was 14.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.410
Taylor Wilson is obsessed with nuclear
fusion.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.800
They didn't get power from the laundry room
now.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:14.090
Taylor passes a high voltage through a small
spherical cage that sits inside the reactor.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:19.640
The negatively charged cage quickly draws
the hydrogen ions inside it.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:23.360
So it's taking all those ions and sucking
them towards the center.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:27.830
And as they fly in, they get confined and
hopefully they collide with each other and

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:28.430
fuse.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:34.670
The temperature of the atoms inside the cage
is now so great that hydrogen atoms are

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:40.700
fusing together, creating heavier helium
atoms and a burst of energy hotter than the

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.110
surface of the sun.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:48.740
It's that little tiny blob of plasma inside
those grid wires that's kind of like a star

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:49.550
in a jar.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.530
13 billion years ago.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:04.590
The universe uses gravity to fuse atoms
instead of an electrical cage.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:11.940
Across the cosmos, vast clouds of hydrogen
gas collapse under their own gravity.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:17.580
Pressure and temperature build as more and
more gas gets sucked in.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:24.360
Eventually, fusion sparks deep inside the
core of these giant balls of gas, and the

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:31.650
first stars start to manufacture many of the
heavy elements that make up your car today.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.580
A star is basically a machine for turning
lighter elements into heavier elements.

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:47.000
Fusion takes place only inside the core of
these first stars, fusing hydrogen atoms

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.130
together to create helium.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:55.460
And when all the hydrogen in the core is
used up, the star finds a new fuel to burn.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:01.680
After you burn hydrogen to form helium, the
core of the star begins to collapse and get

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:07.590
hotter. And there is enough energy then to
fuse three helium nuclei into carbon.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:14.130
And then that fuses to form nitrogen,
oxygen, silicon, iron.

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:20.580
But this incredible production line of
elements can't go on forever.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:24.540
The heavier atoms you ram together, the less
energy you get out.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:26.640
So you turn hydrogen into helium.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.280
Helium becomes carbon, nitrogen, oxygen.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.320
But every time there's a bit less energy to
be had until you get to Ion.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:41.920
The iron that is in your car is actually
essentially a deadly poison when it comes to

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:48.130
a star. It's robbing that star of the heat
needed to keep itself up.

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:55.540
So the star collapses, dies and explodes.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:58.000
At the moment, you create iron in the core.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:00.560
I mean, literally the fraction of a second.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:06.190
I'm not kidding. That's how dramatic and
weird the steel in your car is.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:14.700
The explosion called a supernova, is one of
the brightest and most violent events in the

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:15.630
universe.

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:22.520
It releases enough energy to dwarf what the
sun puts out over its entire lifetime.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:27.230
And all of the elements that it has created
are then dispersed out into space.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:33.050
The gassy remains of the explosion are called
a supernova remnant.

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:38.980
An expanding bubble of hydrogen gas from the
outer layers of the star that mixes with

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:44.500
stardust, the carbon, oxygen, silicon and
iron from its core.

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:51.310
And this same 13 billion year old Stardust
helped you drive to work last week.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:55.250
This was once in the core of a dying star.

00:07:55.250 --> 00:08:00.200
And who knows, maybe some of the iron atoms
in this break disc were forged in the heart

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:03.680
of the very first generation of stars that
illuminated the universe.

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:07.220
When you're pumping iron, you're pumping a
universe.

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:20.340
The first generation of stars created the
materials in a car's chassis, body,

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:22.230
windshield and seats.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:27.810
But we're still missing key components like
the copper for the car's electrics.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:34.470
To create this crucial metal, a new
generation of stars must die an even stranger

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:43.680
death. Picture our universe as the very
first

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:46.560
stars come to the end of their lives.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:55.530
The early Milky Way is filled with flashes
as star after star explodes.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:02.130
These violent supernovas hurl a rich
cocktail of heavy elements into space.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:09.410
The carbon, silicon, aluminum and iron atoms
that will one day build our cars here on

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:10.040
Earth.

